# Render work must stay proportional to its data

Applies to components (`ui/src/**/*.tsx`) and hooks (`ui/src/**/use*.ts`) that
iterate over a collection during render, in a `useMemo`, or in a selector, when
the collection can be large. Large means hundreds of items or more. Examples are
catalogs shipped with the app and lists that grow with a company's data, such
as tasks, runs, agents, connections and activity.

## Why

Render code runs on the main thread, often many times per second while queries
poll or the user types. Work that is quadratic or worse in collection size
looks free with test fixtures of a few items. At real size it can freeze the
tab. Two patterns cause most of these problems:

- **A hidden inner scan.** A loop over one collection calls a helper that scans
  another collection with `some`, `find`, `filter` or `includes`. Each line looks
  cheap, but the cost is the product of both sizes, and nesting multiplies again.
- **A memo that never hits.** A `useMemo` depends on an object, array or `Map`
  that the component body builds again on every render. The memo then
  recomputes on every render, so expensive work runs on each keystroke and
  each poll.

## Requirements

- Do not scan a large collection inside a loop over another large collection.
  Build a `Set` or `Map` index once, outside the loop, and look items up in it.
- Normalize keys (slugs, lowercase names, identities) once per item, not inside
  comparison callbacks.
- Keep every `useMemo` and `useCallback` dependency referentially stable across
  renders that change no data. Memoize derived objects, or derive them from
  query `data`, which TanStack Query keeps structurally shared. `useQueries`
  returns a new array on every render, so do not use it directly as a
  dependency.
- When a change adds or reorders work over a large collection, test that path
  with realistic data size. Load shipped data with `vi.importActual` instead of
  a few hand-written items, and turn on every flag or mode that gates the path.
  Bound the work with a deterministic counter, such as calls to a key
  normalizer, not with wall-clock time. Make the counter throw above its limit,
  so a runaway render fails at once instead of stalling the test worker.

## Exceptions

- Small fixed lists, such as status options, tabs or a few providers.
- A nested loop where both sides are bounded and small, such as one task's
  comments matched against its attachments.

## Review criteria

- Flag nested iteration where both collections can be large, including an
  inner call that hides a scan.
- Flag `useMemo` or `useCallback` dependencies that are rebuilt every render.
- Flag large-collection paths behind a flag or mode whose only tests use a few
  fixture items.
